  "copies": [
    {
      "headline": "Regular Season Nobody, Postseason Everybody",
      "body": "Noah Thesing's whole shtick is that nobody cares about him until December, and that's fine, because John's never actually beaten a live-fire Noah when it mattered. R.I.C.O. bullies Peter for sport (6-3 and counting) but has zero career title runs to show for a 10-4 2025 that everyone in this league still brings up unprompted. Meanwhile Noah just wins rings from the 7 seed and disappears the other ten months.\nProjections have John cruising, 121.6 to 111.9, on the strength of Puka going full alpha and Caleb Williams looking like an actual quarterback now. Noah's counting on Achane and Maye to erase an 18-point deficit in Week 1, which is exactly the kind of math nobody should trust him with in September. Somehow he'll be fine in Week 15 anyway."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Isaac Chases a Ghost, Kaleb Chases Relevance",
      "body": "Isaac hasn't sniffed 11-3 since the Obama-era version of himself started 2022 like a house on fire, and every season since has been a slow letdown by comparison. Four years later he's still the guy at the cabin telling that story nobody asked to hear again, and Week 1 against Kaleb is another chance to either write a new one or confirm he peaked before this league even had a trophy.\nKaleb, meanwhile, has been stuck in the most boring purgatory in the league \u2014 three straight 8-6 seasons since his worst-to-first miracle in 2023, good enough to remember what winning felt like, not good enough to matter in November. Jeanty and Herbert give him the marginally better paper case here, and the projection agrees by two points, but Isaac's whole existence is proving projections wrong for one week and then disappearing for twelve."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Chaos Agent Grant Opens Against Burrowed Treasure",
      "body": "Nobody in the Players League has a wider error bar than Grant. Three-and-eleven, then nine-and-five, then five-and-nine \u2014 that's not a trend, that's a seismograph. \"Amon that inhaler\" could score 150 or could no-show, and the only certainty is that Grant himself has no idea which version shows up. This week he's got Amon-Ra, Hampton, and Baker projected for a combined 52.6, which on paper is fine. On paper is where Grant's team always looks fine.\nCJ, meanwhile, is the answer to \"what if a team just quietly existed.\" One winning season in four, an 8-6 blip in 2024 that nobody remembers, otherwise a permanent tenant below the .500 line. Burrow and Jonathan Taylor give him a real floor, and the projections have this one tight, 120.2 to 115.3. But tight projections and CJ's track record don't mix \u2014 this is the guy who finds a way to be five points short of relevant."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Reigning Champ Walks Into Rezac's House of Pain",
      "body": "Logan finally shed the dress for good in 2025, or so he'd like to think, but the last time he opened a season with a chip on his shoulder he still found a way to finish 4-14 in point differential and top-seed himself into another early exit. Now he gets the actual champion in Week 1, which feels less like a fresh start and more like the universe checking if he's learned anything.\nAdam doesn't care about your redemption arc. He's 34-22 for a reason \u2014 no losing seasons, no drama, just Jaxon Smith-Njigba and Jayden Daniels quietly stacking points while everyone else is busy having a narrative. The projection likes Logan by eight, powered by Hurts and Jefferson going nuclear, and on paper it's close. But Adam's whole career is outscoring his projection when it matters, and Logan's whole career is finding new ways to disappoint in big spots. Vegas says Rezac. History says otherwise."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Peter Opens Year Four Of The Comeback Tour",
      "body": "Peter Lundquist has spent three seasons trying to remember what winning felt like, and he's kicking off attempt number four against a guy whose whole identity is refusing to sit still. Christian's Aura Farm doesn't do middle ground \u2014 it's 10-4 or 4-10, never quietly fine, and Week 1 projections have him as a three-point underdog to a team named after his own worst season.\nPeter's got Bijan and Lamar going full workhorse mode, 19-plus projections apiece, which is the kind of top-end firepower that made 2022 look easy before it very much stopped being easy. Christian counters with CeeDee and Trevor Lawrence, boom-or-bust incarnate leaning on more boom-or-bust incarnate. If Peter wants any of that 13-1 magic back, this is the low-hanging fruit game to start with \u2014 and if Christian wants to prove last year's face-plant was the outlier, not 2024, this is exactly the trap game he needs to steal."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Lunchbox Opens Season Against The Coin Flip",
      "body": "Everyone knows what Leif brings to the table: a losing record, a nickname that's outlived its punchline, and Josh Allen putting up numbers that never seem to matter in the standings. Joey Lunchbox is 22-34 for a reason, and that reason is usually a Week 14 collapse, but Week 1 doesn't care about your history. It just cares whether McCaffrey stays upright.\nMitchell is the league's most boring miracle \u2014 three separate 7-7 seasons like he's contractually obligated to finish exactly average, yet somehow he's got two playoff wins to show for it, more than plenty of teams with better records. Ashton Powers rolls in with Gibbs and a rookie quarterback nobody trusts yet, projected to win by five and some change. Given Mitchell's whole deal, expect this one to land within a field goal either direction."
    }
